y \\(F\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=k=\\frac{AD}{AC},\\qquad \n\\frac{AF}{AD}=k .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3.1) we obtain the useful equalities  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AD\\cdot AC ,\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AD = AD\\cdot AC .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) is cyclic.\n\nFrom step 1 we have \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\).  \nUsing (3.1) we can replace the sides \\(AC,AD\\) by the corresponding segments \\(AE,AF\\) without changing the angles subtended at \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF = \\angle BCD = \\angle BAD = \\angle BAF .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle BAF\\) and \\(\\angle BEF\\) are opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) and they are equal, the quadrilateral is cyclic.  \nDenote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nRecall a standard fact: *In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.*  \n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\).  \nBecause \\(OP\\parallel AB\\) and \\(AB\\perp MN\\), the line \\(OP\\) is **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\); hence \\(OP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nTherefore the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PH'\\); consequently \\(H'\\) is the point of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) diametrically opposite \\(P\\).\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\): both circles pass through the two points \\(M,N\\) and have their centre on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) (the latter is the line \\(OP\\) found in step 2).  \nHence the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite \\(P\\) is exactly \\(A\\) (because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nH' \\equiv A . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5.1) tells us that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is the point \\(A\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection \\(A\\); in other words, it is parallel to the external common tangent to the circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the common point \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).\n\nBoth circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) pass through the point \\(A\\).  \nAt a common point of two circles the tangents coincide; therefore the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) is also the tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\).\n\nFrom step 5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to that common tangent.  \nA line parallel to a tangent of a circle meets the circle at exactly one point (the point of tangency) and therefore is itself a tangent.  \nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), i.e. to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
