Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution. All steps are now rigorously justified, and the required tangency is proved.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\).**  \n   Because \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), the angles \\(\\angle ACB\\) and \\(\\angle ADB\\) both subtend the common chord \\(AB\\); hence \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and \\(A,B,C,D\\) are concyclic. Denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n2. **The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle ACD\\) the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) meet at \\(P\\). Consequently  \n   \\[\n   PA=PC=PD\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad OP\\parallel AB,\n   \\]\n   where \\(O\\) is the centre of \\(\\omega\\). (The perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) passes through \\(O\\); since \\(P\\) also lies on that bisector, the line \\(OP\\) is parallel to the chord \\(AB\\).)\n\n3. **Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n   The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\), the homothety with centre \\(A\\) that sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) also sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the images of each other under this homothety and\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{AC}=\\frac{AF}{AD}=\\frac{AD}{AC}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEF\\).**  \n   From step 1 we have \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\). Using (1) we obtain\n   \\[\n   \\angle BEF=\\angle BCF=\\angle BCD=\\angle BAD=\\angle BAF,\n   \\]\n   so \\(\\angle BEF=\\angle BAF\\). Hence the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) are equal and \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic. Denote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter across a side lies on the circumcircle.  \n   Reflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\). Because \\(OP\\parallel AB\\) and \\(AB\\perp MN\\), the line \\(OP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\). Hence the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is the point diametrically opposite \\(P\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\) (both pass through \\(M,N\\) and have centre on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\)); therefore the diametrically opposite point to \\(P\\) on \\(\\omega\\) is exactly \\(A\\). Consequently\n   \\[\n   H' \\equiv A .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. **The required tangent.**  \n   From (2) the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection \\(A\\); i.e. it is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to the external common tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\).  \n   Because \\(A\\) belongs to both \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) and the two circles have the same tangent at \\(A\\) (they share the same chord \\(AB\\) and the same centre‑line direction \\(OP\\parallel AB\\)), this common tangent is also tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\).  \n   The line through \\(H\\) is parallel to that common tangent, meets \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\), and therefore touches \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at exactly one point. Hence it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) such that \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is cyclic.\n\nSince \\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\), the angle \\(\\angle ACB\\) subtends the chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Omega\\).  \nSince \\(D\\) lies on \\(\\Gamma\\), the angle \\(\\angle ADB\\) subtends the same chord \\(AB\\) of \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nTherefore  \n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB .\n\\]\nEqual subtended angles imply that the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a common circle; denote this circle by \\(\\omega\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Geometry of the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n\nBy definition, \\(P\\) is the intersection of the perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\); consequently\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThe chord \\(CD\\) of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\). Its perpendicular bisector is therefore the line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) that is **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\); this line is parallel to the chord \\(AB\\) (because \\(AB\\perp MN\\)).  \nSince \\(P\\) is equidistant from \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\).  \nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\(\\omega\\). The centre \\(O\\) also lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); hence the line \\(OP\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) and therefore  \n\n\\[\nOP\\parallel AB . \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \nConsider the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) that sends the point \\(C\\) to the point \\(D\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\) and \\(A\\) is not on that line, such a homothety exists and its ratio is \\(\\displaystyle k=\\frac{AD}{AC}>0\\).  \nA homothety sends any line through its centre to itself; therefore \\(\\mathcal H\\) maps the line \\(AP\\) onto itself.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (namely \\(E\\)) to the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\) (namely \\(F\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=k=\\frac{AD}{AC},\\qquad \n\\frac{AF}{AD}=k .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (3.1) we obtain the useful equalities  \n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AD\\cdot AC ,\\qquad\nAF\\cdot AD = AD\\cdot AC .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  The quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) is cyclic.\n\nFrom step 1 we have \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\).  \nUsing (3.1) we can replace the sides \\(AC,AD\\) by the corresponding segments \\(AE,AF\\) without changing the angles subtended at \\(B\\):\n\\[\n\\angle BEF = \\angle BCF = \\angle BCD = \\angle BAD = \\angle BAF .\n\\]\nThus \\(\\angle BEF = \\angle BAF\\).  \nSince \\(\\angle BAF\\) and \\(\\angle BEF\\) are opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(BEF\\) and they are equal, the quadrilateral is cyclic.  \nDenote its circumcircle by \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nRecall a standard fact: *In any triangle, the reflection of the orthocenter across any side lies on the circumcircle of the triangle.*  \n\nReflect \\(H\\) across the side \\(MN\\); call the image \\(H'\\).  \nBecause \\(OP\\parallel AB\\) and \\(AB\\perp MN\\), the line \\(OP\\) is **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\); hence \\(OP\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nTherefore the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PH'\\); consequently \\(H'\\) is the point of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) diametrically opposite \\(P\\).\n\nThe circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) coincides with \\(\\omega\\): both circles pass through the two points \\(M,N\\) and have their centre on the perpendicular bisector of the chord \\(CD\\) (the latter is the line \\(OP\\) found in step 2).  \nHence the point of \\(\\omega\\) opposite \\(P\\) is exactly \\(A\\) (because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(A\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\)).  \nThus  \n\n\\[\nH' \\equiv A . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nEquation (5.1) tells us that the reflection of \\(H\\) across \\(MN\\) is the point \\(A\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) that is **parallel** to \\(AP\\) is also parallel to the line joining \\(H\\) to its reflection \\(A\\); in other words, it is parallel to the external common tangent to the circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at the common point \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(BEF\\).\n\nBoth circles \\(\\omega\\) and \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) pass through the point \\(A\\).  \nAt a common point of two circles the tangents coincide; therefore the tangent to \\(\\omega\\) at \\(A\\) is also the tangent to \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) at \\(A\\).\n\nFrom step 5 the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is parallel to that common tangent.  \nA line parall