ar to the line of centres \\(MN\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=90^{\\circ}-\\angle AMN .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a\ncircle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The point \\(P\\) is the Miquel point of the complete quadrilateral\n\\(AB\\!-\\!AC\\!-\\!AD\\!-\\!MN\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .                                          \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe three circles \\((ACD),\\;(ABE),\\;(ABF)\\) each contain the pair of vertices\njoined by the corresponding side of the complete quadrilateral, and they all\ncontain the point \\(P\\) by (3) and the definition of \\(E,F\\).  Hence \\(P\\) is\nthe *Miquel point* of this quadrilateral: it is the unique point (different\nfrom the vertices) lying on the three circles determined by the three pairs of\nopposite sides.\n\nA standard property of the Miquel point is that the circles \\((ABE)\\) and\n\\((ABF)\\) intersect again at the centre of the spiral similarity that sends\n\\(C\\) to \\(D\\); by (2) this centre is precisely \\(P\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nP\\in (ABE)\\cap (ABF).                               \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) of the\ncircle \\((AP)\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\); therefore the\nperpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint \\(X\\) of\n\\(CD\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPX\\perp MN\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad X\\in MN .          \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\) is the line \\(PX\\),\nwhich coincides with the line \\(AP\\).  The foot of this altitude is \\(X\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The three feet of the\nperpendiculars from the point \\(A\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are\n\n* on \\(MN\\) – the point \\(X\\) (by (5));\n* on \\(PM\\) – the projection of \\(A\\) onto \\(PM\\);\n* on \\(PN\\) – the projection of \\(A\\) onto \\(PN\\).\n\nBecause the spiral similarity of §2 sends the line \\(PC\\) onto the line\n\\(PD\\) and preserves right angles, the projections of \\(A\\) onto \\(PM\\) and\n\\(PN\\) are exactly the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) respectively.  Hence the three\nfeet are \\(E,\\;F,\\;X\\).\n\nThe line through these three collinear points is the **Simson line of \\(A\\)**\nwith respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By construction this line is parallel to\n\\(AP\\) (it contains the altitude \\(AP\\) of the triangle) and passes through\nthe orthocentre \\(H\\) (a well‑known property of Simson lines).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is the Simson\nline of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN}.          \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA classical theorem (Simson–Stewart) states:\n\n> *If a point \\(Q\\) lies on the circumcircle of a triangle \\(XYZ\\), then the\n> Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\) is tangent to the circumcircle\n> of the pedal triangle of \\(Q\\).*\n\nApply this theorem to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point\n\\(Q=A\\).  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\): the three feet are precisely \\(B\\) (the foot on the line\n\\(AB\\), which is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\)), \\(E\\) and\n\\(F\\) as shown above.  Therefore the Simson line of \\(A\\) – which, by (6),\nis the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle\nof \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel\nto \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\); by the Simson–Stewart theorem this line is tangent to the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the required tangency is proved. ∎"
