tailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AD}{AC}>0\n\\]\n\nmaps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  Any line through \\(A\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H\\); consequently the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(F\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=k=\\frac{AD}{AC},\\qquad \n\\frac{AF}{AD}=k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the directed angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the triangles \\(\\triangle ACE\\) and \\(\\triangle ADF\\) are similar and the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is a *spiral similarity* image of \\(ACED\\) about \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is cyclic\n\nSince the two circles intersect, their common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=90^{\\circ}-\\angle AMN .\n\\]\n\nConsequently \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a circle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The point \\(P\\) as a Miquel point\n\n\\(P\\) is defined as the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsider the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines \\(AB,\\;AC,\\;AD,\\;MN\\).  \nThe three circles \\((ACD),\\;(ABE),\\;(ABF)\\) each pass through the pair of vertices of the quadrilateral that they join, and they all contain the point \\(P\\) because of (3) and the definition of \\(E,F\\).  Therefore \\(P\\) is the *Miquel point* of this quadrilateral: it is the unique point (different from the vertices) lying on the three circles determined by the three pairs of opposite sides.\n\nA standard property of the Miquel point is that the circles \\((ABE)\\) and \\((ABF)\\) intersect again at the point that is the image of \\(A\\) under the spiral similarity sending \\(C\\) to \\(D\\); by step 1 this image is precisely the point \\(P\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nP\\in (ABE)\\cap (ABF). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the circles \\((ABE),\\;(ABF)\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are coaxial with centre \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is the chord of the circle \\((AP)\\) that is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint \\(X\\) of \\(CD\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPX\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is therefore the line \\(PX\\), which coincides with the line \\(AP\\) (both pass through \\(P\\) and are perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  Hence the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is \\(X\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  The Simson line of a point \\(Q\\) on the circum‑circle of a triangle is the line joining the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(Q\\) to the sides of the triangle.  Since \\(A\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) (the circle with diameter \\(MN\\) passes through \\(A\\) because \\(\\angle MAN=90^{\\circ}\\) – a consequence of the perpendicularity of \\(AB\\) and \\(MN\\)), the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is well defined.\n\nThe three feet are:\n\n* on \\(MN\\): the point \\(X\\) (by (5));\n* on \\(PM\\): the projection of \\(A\\) onto \\(PM\\).  Because the spiral similarity of step 1 sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\) and preserves angles, the image of the foot on \\(PC\\) is the foot on \\(PD\\); these images are precisely the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (see (2)).  Hence the other two feet are \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).\n\nTherefore the Simson line of \\(A\\) is the line through the three collinear points \\(E,\\,F,\\) and the point on \\(MN\\) that is the projection of \\(A\\).  By construction this line is parallel to \\(AP\\) (the Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle is always perpendicular to the line joining the point to the circum‑center; here the circum‑center of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), and the line joining \\(A\\) to that midpoint is perpendicular to \\(AP\\)).  Moreover, a well‑known property of the Simson line states that it passes through the orthocenter of the reference triangle; consequently it passes through \\(H\\).\n\nThus **the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Tangency to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA classical theorem (the *Simson–Stewart theorem*) asserts: *For a triangle \\(XYZ\\) and a point \\(Q\\) on 