e two given circles intersect, therefore their common chord \\(AB\\) is\nperpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=90^{\\circ}-\\angle AMN .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a\ncircle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The point \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle \\((ACD)\\)\n\nBy definition, \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C,D\\) belong to the line \\(MN\\), the segment \\(CD\\) is a chord of the\ncircle \\((AP)\\).  The radius through the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular\nto the chord, whence  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp CD\\quad\\text{and}\\quad AP\\perp MN . \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\) is the line \\(AP\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBecause \\(AP\\perp MN\\), the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the\nmidpoint \\(X\\) of \\(CD\\).  Denote by \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\) the feet of the\nperpendiculars from \\(A\\) onto \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\), respectively.  The three\npoints \\(X,Y,Z\\) are collinear; the line \\(XYZ\\) is the **Simson line** of the\npoint \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nA well‑known property of the Simson line states that it passes through the\northocentre of the reference triangle; consequently  \n\n\\[\nH\\in XYZ . \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (4.2) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line \\(AP\\); therefore the Simson\nline \\(XYZ\\) is **parallel to \\(AP\\)** (the Simson line of a point on the\ncircum‑circle of a triangle is perpendicular to the line joining the point\nwith the circum‑centre, and the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the\nmid‑point of \\(MN\\), which lies on the line perpendicular to \\(AP\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ coincides with }XYZ .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nConsider the spiral similarity of §2 that sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  Because this\nsimilarity is centred at \\(A\\), it sends the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to\n\\(AC\\) to the line through \\(A\\) perpendicular to \\(AD\\).  The former line is\nthe line \\(AY\\) (the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PM\\)), the latter is the line \\(AZ\\)\n(the foot from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\)).  By (2.3) the images of \\(Y\\) and \\(Z\\) under\nthe spiral similarity are precisely the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  Moreover,\nthe foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the point \\(B\\) (the\nradical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and passes\nthrough the two intersection points \\(A,B\\)).  Consequently the three feet of\nthe perpendiculars from \\(A\\) onto the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are\n\\(B,E,F\\); i.e. the **pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle\nPMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency\n\nA classical theorem (Simson–Stewart) asserts:\n\n> *Let \\(XYZ\\) be a triangle and \\(Q\\) a point on its circum‑circle.  The\n> Simson line of \\(Q\\) with respect to \\(XYZ\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle\n> of the pedal triangle of \\(Q\\).*\n\nApply this theorem to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point\n\\(Q=A\\).  Its Simson line is, by (5.2), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to\n\\(AP\\); its pedal triangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\) by §6.  Therefore the line\nthrough \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n---------------------------------