\nperpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB=90^{\\circ}-\\angle AMN .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and the four points \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a\ncircle; denote it by \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The point \\(P\\) is the Miquel point of the complete quadrilateral  \n\nConsider the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines  \n\n\\[\nAB,\\qquad AC,\\qquad AD,\\qquad MN .\n\\]\n\nThe three circles \\((ACD),\\;(ABE),\\;(ABF)\\) each pass through the two vertices\njoined by the corresponding pair of opposite sides, and they all contain the\npoint \\(P\\) because of (1) and the definition of \\(P\\) (\\(PA=PC=PD\\)).\nTherefore \\(P\\) is the **Miquel point** of this quadrilateral; in particular  \n\n\\[\nP\\in (ABE)\\cap (ABF). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the circles \\((ABE),\\;(ABF)\\) and \\((BEF)\\) are coaxial with\ncentre \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) and the reflection of \\(A\\) in \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(CD\\).  \nSince \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) and \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is\nperpendicular to the radius \\(PA\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nPX\\perp MN .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the altitude of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) issued from \\(P\\) is the line \\(PX\\).\nThe foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) onto \\(MN\\) is exactly the point \\(X\\).\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  In any triangle the\nreflection of the orthocentre in a side lies on the circum‑circle.  Reflect\n\\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\); denote the image by \\(A'\\).  Because the reflection\nof the altitude \\(PX\\) in \\(MN\\) is the line through \\(P\\) symmetric to\n\\(PX\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA'=PA .\n\\]\n\nMoreover \\(A'\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Comparing the\ndistances \\(PA'=PA=PC=PD\\) we see that the only point on that circum‑circle\nhaving the same distance to \\(P\\) as \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) is the point \\(A\\)\nitself.  Hence  \n\n\\[\nA'=A .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus **\\(A\\) is the reflection of the orthocentre \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\)**.\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nMH=MA\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad NH=NA .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we also obtain that \\(A\\) belongs to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\); therefore the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) is well defined.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  The Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the Simson line is the line\nthrough the three feet of the perpendiculars dropped from the point to the\nsides of the triangle.  \n\n* The foot on \\(MN\\) is the midpoint \\(X\\) of \\(CD\\) (by (4)).  \n* Because the spiral similarity \\(\\mathcal S_{A}\\) sends the line \\(PC\\) onto\n\\(PD\\) and preserves right angles, the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to\n\\(PM\\) is carried to the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(PN\\); these\ntwo feet are precisely the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) (see (2)).  \n\nHence the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line\nthrough the three collinear points  \n\n\\[\nE,\\;F,\\;X .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\) and \\(A\\) is the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(MN\\),\nthe Simson line passes through the orthocentre \\(H\\) and is parallel to the\nline \\(AP\\) (the Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle is always\nperpendicular to the line joining the point with the circum‑centre; the\ncircum‑centre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\), so the Simson\nline is parallel to \\(AP\\)).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nL\\;=\\;\\text{the Simson line of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }\\triangle PMN .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Tangency to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nA classical theorem (Simson–Stewart) states:\n\n> *For a triangle \\(XYZ\\) and a point \\(Q\\) on its circum‑circle, the Simson\n> line of \\(Q\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle of\n> \\(Q\\).*\n\nIn our situation the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle\nPMN\\) is exactly \\(\\triangle BEF\\): the three feet are \\(E\\) (on \\(PM\\)),\n\\(F\\) (on \\(PN\\)) and the foot on \\(MN\\), which lies on the line \\(AB\\); the\npoint where this foot meets \\(AB\\) is \\(B\\) because \\(AB\\) is the radical axis\nof the two original circles and is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  Hence the pedal\ntriangle is \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nApplying the Simson–Stewart theorem we obtain that the Simson line of \\(A\\)\nwith respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) – which, by (7), is precisely the line\n\\(L\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel\nto \\(AP\\) coincides with the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\); by the Simson–Stewart theorem this line is tangent to the\ncircum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Hence the required tangency holds.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to }(BEF).}\n\\]"
