ateral \\(ABCD\\).**  \n   Since the two given circles intersect, their common chord \\(AB\\) is perpendicular to the line of centres \\(MN\\); therefore \\(\\angle ACB=\\angle ADB\\) and \\(A,B,C,D\\) lie on a circle \\(\\omega\\).\n\n3. **The point \\(P\\) as the Miquel point.**  \n   Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑center of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  Consequently \\(P\\) is the *Miquel point* of the complete quadrilateral formed by the lines \\(AB,\\,AC,\\,AD,\\) and the line \\(MN\\).  In particular the circles \\((ACD),\\,(ABE),\\,(ABF)\\) all pass through \\(P\\).\n\n4. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) and the Simson line of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Because \\(PA=PC=PD\\) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), this altitude is exactly the line \\(AP\\).  Hence the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) on \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of \\(CD\\); denote it by \\(X\\).  The Simson line of the point \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the line through the projections of \\(A\\) onto the three sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  One of these projections is precisely the foot \\(X\\) on \\(MN\\); the other two lie on the lines through \\(A\\) parallel to \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\).  It follows that the Simson line of \\(A\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) and passes through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n5. **Tangency.**  \n   The Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle is tangent to the circum‑circle of the pedal triangle of that point.  Here the pedal triangle of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because the spiral similarity of step 1 sends the feet on \\(PM\\) and \\(PN\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)).  Therefore the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – which is the Simson line of \\(A\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is established without invoking the erroneous identification of circles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) be the circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\);  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) be the points on the line \\(MN\\) with the order \\(C-M-N-D\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Spiral similarity centred at \\(A\\)\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AD}{AC}>0\n\\]\n\nmaps \\(C\\) to \\(D\\).  Any line through \\(A\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal H\\); consequently the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point \\(E\\)) is sent to the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point \\(F\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AC}=k=\\frac{AD}{AC},\\qquad \n\\frac{AF}{AD}=k .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus the directed angles satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAE=\\angle DAF . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn particular the triangles \\(\\triangle ACE\\) and \\(\\triangle ADF\\) are similar and the quadrilateral \\(AEFD\\) is a *spiral similarity* image of \\(ACED\\) about \\(A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is cyclic\n\nSince 