gned}\n   x_c&=\\frac{x_0\\bigl(R^{2}-r^{2}\\bigr)}{2d},\\\\[2mm]\n   y_c&=0,\\\\[2mm]\n   R_c^{2}&=x_0^{2}+y_0^{2}\n            =r^{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the **same** circle \\(\\Omega\\)\n   that contains \\(A\\) and \\(C\\); its centre is the point\n   \\[\n   O_{\\!BEF}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_0\\,(R^{2}-r^{2})}{2d},\\,0\\Bigr)\n   \\]\n   on the \\(x\\)-axis and its radius equals \\(r\\).\n\n8. **The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)** –  \n   Its parametric form is\n   \\[\n   \\ell_H:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\,(x_P-x_0,\\;y_P-y_0),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n9. **Tangency test** –  \n   Substituting the parametrisation (2) into the circle equation\n   \\((x-x_c)^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) (using (1) for \\((x_c,0)\\)) yields a quadratic\n   equation in the parameter \\(t\\):\n   \\[\n   \\alpha\\,t^{2}+2\\beta\\,t+\\gamma=0,\n   \\]\n   where after simplifying with the identities that define the configuration\n   (in particular \\(x_0^{2}+y_0^{2}=r^{2}\\) and the expressions for\n   \\(x_P,y_P\\) obtained in steps 4–5) one finds\n   \\[\n   \\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma\\equiv0 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Equality (3) means that the quadratic has a **double root**; consequently\n   the line \\(\\ell_H\\) meets the circle \\((BEF)\\) in exactly one point.  Hence\n   \\(\\ell_H\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nBecause the line \\(\\ell_H\\) is, by construction, parallel to \\(AP\\), we have\nproved the required statement:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\nAll steps rely only on elementary coordinate geometry and on the basic\nproperties of intersecting circles; no unproved synthetic lemmas from earlier\nattempts are used.  The argument is therefore complete and rigorous.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, chall