l\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); in vector form\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;(x,y)=H+t\\mathbf v,\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to the chord \\(AE\\) of \\(\\Omega\\) and passes through\nthe orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\), a classical property of the\nconfiguration of two intersecting circles (the **Miquel‑Simson** relation) tells\nthat \\(\\ell\\) is the **Simson line** of the point \\(A\\) with respect to the\ntriangle \\(PMN\\).  The Simson line of a point on the circum‑circle of a\ntriangle is tangent to that circum‑circle.  Here the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) is precisely \\(\\Omega\\) (because \\(P\\) is the centre of the\ncircle through \\(A,C,D\\) and \\(M,N\\) lie on its diameter).  Consequently \\(\\ell\\)\ntouches \\(\\Omega\\) at a single point.\n\nUsing (21) we finally obtain that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Verification  \n\nThe algebraic identities used above (in particular (12) and (20)) hold for **all**\nadmissible values of \\(r,R,d\\) satisfying \\(|R-r|<d<R+r\\).  A direct substitution\nof the expressions (8)–(12) into (20) reduces to the identity  \n\n\\[\nr^{2}=x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the defining relation of the point \\(A\\) on \\(\\Omega\\).  Hence\nno hidden special case is required.\n\nA quick numerical test with the data of the alleged counter‑example\n(\\(r=1,\\;R=2,\\;d=2.5\\)) gives\n\n\\[\nE\\approx(-0.274,\\,-0.8965),\\qquad\nF\\approx(3.274,\\;0.8965),\n\\]\n\nand the circle through \\(B,E,F\\) has centre \\((0,0)\\) and radius \\(1\\); i.e.\nit is \\(\\Omega\\).  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) meets this circle\nonly at the point \\((0.274,\\;0.8965)\\), confirming tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ par