es through the centre $P$ of the inversion, so it is invariant: $\\mathcal I(AP)=AP$.\n\n*The images of $E$ and $F$.*  \n$E$ (resp. $F$) is the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$ (resp. $\\Gamma$). Hence $E'=\\mathcal I(E)$ (resp. $F'=\\mathcal I(F)$) is the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega'$ (resp. $\\Gamma'$). Consequently\n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap\\Omega',\\qquad F' = AP\\cap\\Gamma'.\n\\]\n\nBoth $E'$ and $F'$ lie on $AP$.\n\n*The image of $B$.*  \n$B$ is the second intersection of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$. Its image $B'=\\mathcal I(B)$ is the intersection of $\\Omega'$ and $\\Gamma'$, i.e. the point at infinity in the direction $AB$. Hence $B'$ is the same point at infinity as $A'$.\n\n*The image of the circumcircle $(BEF)$.*  \nSince $(BEF)$ passes through $B$, whose image is a point at infinity, the image of $(BEF)$ is a line. This line contains $E'$ and $F'$, and is parallel to $AB$ (the direction of the point at infinity). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=E'F',\\qquad E'F'\\parallel AB\\perp MN .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocentre $H$ and its image $H'$\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$ the altitude from $P$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$; by definition of the orthocentre, $PH\\perp MN$. Since $AB\\perp MN$, we have\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying the inversion, $H$ maps to a point $H'$ on the ray $PH$ (inversion preserves the direction of a line through the centre). Because $PH\\perp MN$, the image $PH'$ is also perpendicular to $MN$, and by (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AB\\parallel E'F'.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$\n\nLet $\\ell$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$. Since $\\ell$ does **not** pass through the centre $P$, its image $\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)$ is a circle passing through $P$ and $H'$. Moreover, because