tangent to a line $l$ at $P$ has its centre on the line through $P$ perpendicular to $l$.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ be the circles with centres $M$ and $N$, respectively, intersecting at $A$ and $B$;  \n* $C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN$, $D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN$ with $C,M,N,D$ in this order;  \n* $P$ be the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$, i.e. $PA=PC=PD$;  \n* $E\\neq A$ and $F\\neq A$ be the second intersections of the line $AP$ with $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, respectively;  \n* $H$ be the orthocentre of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\nWe must prove that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle $(BEF)$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion about $P$\n\nConsider the inversion $\\mathcal I$ with centre $P$ and power $R^{2}=PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}$.  \nSince $C$ and $D$ lie on the inversion circle, they are fixed by $\\mathcal I$.\n\n*The images of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$.*  \nBoth $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ pass through a point of the inversion circle ($C$ and $D$, respectively). By the inversion lemma, each is mapped to the tangent line at that point. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\Omega'=\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\text{tangent to }\\Omega\\text{ at }C,\\qquad\n\\Gamma'=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\text{tangent to }\\Gamma\\text{ at }D .\n\\]\n\nSince $MC$ and $ND$ are collinear with $MN$, the radii $MC$ and $ND$ are parallel to $MN$; therefore the tangents at $C$ and $D$ are both perpendicular to $MN$. Consequently $\\Omega'$ and $\\Gamma'$ are two distinct parallel lines, both orthogonal to $MN$.\n\n*The image of $A$.*  \n$A$ belongs to both $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, hence its image $A'=\\mathcal I(A)$ belongs to both $\\Omega'$ and $\\Gamma'$. As $\\Omega'$ and $\\Gamma'$ are parallel, $A'$ is the point at infinity in the direction orthogonal to $MN$, i.e. the direction of the common chord $AB$. Thus $A'$ is the point at infinity on the line $AB$.\n\n*The image of $AP$.*  \nThe line $AP$ passes through the centre $P$ of the inversion, so it is invariant: $\\mathcal I(AP)=AP$.\n\n*The images of $E$ and $F$.*  \n$E$ (resp. $F$) is the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega$ (resp. $\\Gamma$). Hence $E'=\\mathcal I(E)$ (resp. $F'=\\mathcal I(F)$) is the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Omega'$ (resp. $\\Gamma'$). Consequently\n\n\\[\nE' = AP\\cap\\Omega',\\qquad F' = AP\\cap\\Gamma'.\n\\]\n\nBoth $E'$ and $F'$ lie on $AP$.\n\n*The image of $B$.*  \n$B$ is the second intersection of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$. Its image $B'=\\mathcal I(B)$ is the intersection of $\\Omega'$ and $\\Gamma'$, i.e. the point at infinity in the direction $AB$. Hence $B'$ is the same point at infinity as $A'$.\n\n*The image of the circumcircle $(BEF)$.*  \nSince $(BEF)$ passes through $B$, whose image is a point at infinity, the image of $(BEF)$ is a line. This line contains $E'$ and $F'$, and is parallel to $AB$ (the direction of the point at infinity). Hence\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=E'F',\\qquad E'F'\\parallel AB\\perp MN .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocentre $H$ and its image $H'$\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$ the altitude from $P$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to $MN$; by definition of the orthocentre, $PH\\perp MN$. Since $AB\\perp MN$, we have\n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nApplying the inversion, $H$ maps to a point $H'$ on the ray $PH$ (inversion preserves the direction of a line through the centre). Because $PH\\perp MN$, the image $PH'$ is also perpendicular to $MN$, and by (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AB\\parallel E'F'.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$\n\nLet $\\ell$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$. Since $\\ell$ does **not** pass through the centre $P$, its image $\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)$ is a circle passing through $P$ and $H'$. Moreover, because