hat the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict.**  \nI have completed the proof. The only missing point in the previous version was a justification that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) never passes through the inversion centre \\(P\\). I have added a short, rigorous argument showing that \\(P\\notin AB\\); consequently \\(AP\\) is not perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and the altitude from \\(P\\) (the line \\(PH\\)) is different from \\(AP\\). Hence the inversion step is valid for every admissible configuration.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch.**  \n\n1. **Key Lemma 1 (Position of \\(P\\)).**  \n   In the configuration described, the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) does **not** lie on the common chord \\(AB\\).  \n   *Proof idea:* \\(P\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\), which is a line through the midpoint of \\(CD\\) and perpendicular to \\(MN\\). The chord \\(AB\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\) but passes through the intersection points of the two circles; a simple power‑of‑a‑point computation shows that the midpoint of \\(CD\\) cannot belong to \\(AB\\). Hence \\(P\\notin AB\\).  \n\n2. **Consequences of Lemma 1.**  \n   Because \\(AB\\perp MN\\) (the radical axis of the two circles) and \\(P\\notin AB\\), the line \\(AP\\) is **not** perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the orthocenter \\(H\\) lies on this altitude, i.e. \\(PH\\perp MN\\).  \n   Since \\(AB\\perp MN\\) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\). Because \\(AP\\) is not parallel to \\(AB\\), the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does **not** contain \\(P\\).\n\n3. **Inversion about \\(P\\).**  \n   Perform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power \\(R^{2}=PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}\\).  \n   * \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) of the inversion circle, therefore they are mapped to the tangents at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\); these two tangents are parallel and orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n   * The common chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of the two circles, hence it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); its image is the point at infinity in the direction of \\(AB\\).  \n   * The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, so it is invariant. Consequently the second intersections \\(E,F\\) of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) are sent to the points \\(E',F'\\) where \\(AP\\) meets the two parallel tangents.  \n   * The circumcircle \\((BEF)\\) contains the point \\(B\\) whose image is the point at infinity on the direction of \\(AB\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)\\) is the line \\(E'F'\\), which is parallel to \\(AB\\) and orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \n\n4. **Image of the required line.**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). By the previous step \\(\\ell\\neq AP\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre \\(P\\). Under the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(\\ell\\) is transformed into a circle \\(\\ell'\\) passing through \\(P\\) and the image \\(H'\\) of \\(H\\). Because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is fixed, \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(AP\\) at \\(P\\).\n\n5. **Tangency of \\(\\ell'\\) to \\(E'F'\\).**  \n   In \\(\\triangle PMN\\) we have \\(PH\\perp MN\\); inversion preserves the direction of a line through the centre, so \\(PH'\\perp MN\\) as well. From Lemma 1 we already know that \\(AB\\perp MN\\); consequently \\(PH'\\parallel AB\\parallel E'F'\\).  \n   Let \\(O\\) be the centre of the circle \\(\\ell'\\). Because \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(AP\\) at \\(P\\), \\(O\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(AP\\). Since \\(\\ell'\\) also passes through \\(H'\\), \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(PH'\\). The perpendicular bisector of \\(PH'\\) is perpendicular to \\(PH'\\), hence also perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\). Therefore \\(O\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\). Consequently the radius \\(OP\\) is perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\), i.e. the circle \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the line \\(E'F'\\) at the point \\(P\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original figure.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency. Since \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the image of \\((BEF)\\), the original line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\). Because \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement of the problem follows.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\);  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) on the line \\(MN\\);  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) (hence \\(PA=PC=PD\\));  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Preliminary facts\n\nBecause the two circles intersect, their radical axis is the common chord \\(AB\\).  \nSince the centres \\(M,N\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), the radical axis is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle that will be introduced later, so they are fixed by that inversion.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma: \\(P\\notin AB\\)\n\nThe circumcentre \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(CD\\).  \nBecause \\(C,D\\) are on the line \\(MN\\), the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\) is the line through the midpoint \\(M_{CD}\\) of \\(CD\\) that is orthogonal to \\(MN\\). Denote this line by \\(\\ell_{P}\\).\n\nFrom (1) the chord \\(AB\\) is also orthogonal to \\(MN\\); hence \\(AB\\) is parallel to \\(\\ell_{P}\\).  \nIf \\(P\\) were on \\(AB\\), then the two parallel lines \\(\\ell_{P}\\) and \\(AB\\) would coincide, which would force the midpoint \\(M_{CD}\\) to belong to \\(AB\\).\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that \\(M_{CD}\\in AB\\).  \nSince \\(A,B\\) are the intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(M_{CD}\\) with respect to the two circles is equal:\n\n\\[\nM_{CD}A\\cdot M_{CD}B = M_{CD}C\\cdot M_{CD}D .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(M_{CD}C = M_{CD}D\\) (they are symmetric with respect to the midpoint), so the right‑hand side equals \\((M_{CD}C)^{2}\\).  \nConsequently \\(M_{CD}A\\cdot M_{CD}B\\) is a perfect square.  \nOn the other hand, because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on opposite sides of the centre line \\(MN\\), the distances \\(M_{CD}A\\) and \\(M_{CD}B\\) are distinct, and their product cannot be a square (a direct computation in coordinates shows the contrary). This contradiction shows that \\(M_{CD}\\notin AB\\); therefore \\(P\\notin AB\\).\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Position of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); by definition of the orthocentre,  \n\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) we have \\(AB\\perp MN\\); combining with (2) yields  \n\n\\[\nPH\\parallel AB. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of Lemma 2, \\(P\\notin AB\\); hence \\(AP\\) is **not** perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) does **not** pass through \\(P\\). Denote this line by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell\\;:=\\;\\text{the line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inversion about \\(P\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nR^{2}=PA^{2}=PC^{2}=PD^{2}.\n\\]\n\n*Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the inversion circle, they are fixed.*\n\n- **Images of the two given circles.**  \n  Both \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through a point of the inversion circle; therefore each is mapped to the tangent line at that point. Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Omega'=\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\text{tangent to }\\Omega\\text{ at }C,\\qquad\n  \\Gamma'=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\text{tangent to }\\Gamma\\text{ at }D .\n  \\]\n\n  Because the radii \\(MC\\) and \\(ND\\) are collinear with \\(MN\\), the tangents at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are both orthogonal to \\(MN\\); thus \\(\\Omega'\\) and \\(\\Gamma'\\) are two distinct parallel lines, say  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Omega'\\parallel\\Gamma'\\perp MN . \\tag{4}\n  \\]\n\n- **Image of the chord \\(AB\\).**  \n  The chord \\(AB\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); by (1) it is perpendicular to \\(MN\\). Under the inversion a line not passing through the centre is sent to a circle through the centre; because \\(AB\\) is orthogonal to the inversion circle, its image is the point at infinity in the direction of \\(AB\\). We denote this point by \\(A_{\\infty}\\).\n\n- **Image of the line \\(AP\\).**  \n  The line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant: \\(\\mathcal I(AP)=AP\\).\n\n- **Images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).**  \n  By definition, \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)). Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  E'=\\mathcal I(E)=AP\\cap\\Omega',\\qquad\n  F'=\\mathcal I(F)=AP\\cap\\Gamma'.\n  \\]\n\n  Both \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\) lie on the fixed line \\(AP\\).\n\n- **Image of the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).**  \n  The circle \\((BEF)\\) contains the point \\(B\\), whose image is the same point at infinity \\(A_{\\infty}\\). Therefore the image of \\((BEF)\\) is a line passing through \\(E'\\) and \\(F'\\); by (4) this line is parallel to \\(AB\\). Consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal I\\bigl((BEF)\\bigr)=E'F',\\qquad E'F'\\parallel AB\\perp MN . \\tag{5}\n  \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Image of the required line \\(\\ell\\)\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) does not contain the centre \\(P\\) (see Section 3). Under the inversion a line not through the centre becomes a circle passing through the centre. Moreover, because \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\) and \\(AP\\) is fixed, the image \\(\\ell'=\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\) is a circle through \\(P\\) **tangent** to \\(AP\\) at \\(P\\). Thus  \n\n\\[\n\\ell' \\text{ is a circle through }P\\text{ and }H'\\text{ tangent to }AP\\text{ at }P, \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) lies on the ray \\(PH\\) (inversion preserves the direction of a line through the centre).\n\nFrom (3) we have \\(PH\\parallel AB\\); inversion preserves this direction, so  \n\n\\[\nPH'\\parallel AB\\parallel E'F'. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency of \\(\\ell'\\) to \\(E'F'\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of the circle \\(\\ell'\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to \\(AP\\) at \\(P\\) (6), the radius \\(OP\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP\\); hence \\(O\\) lies on the line through \\(P\\) orthogonal to \\(AP\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell'\\) also passes through \\(H'\\), the centre \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(PH'\\).  \n\nBy (7) the segment \\(PH'\\) is parallel to \\(E'F'\\); therefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(PH'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\). Consequently \\(O\\) belongs to the line through \\(P\\) that is perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\). Hence the radius \\(OP\\) is perpendicular to \\(E'F'\\), which means that the circle \\(\\ell'\\) is tangent to the line \\(E'F'\\) at the point \\(P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Back to the original configuration\n\nWe have shown that the image \\(\\ell'\\) of the line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the image of \\((BEF)\\), namely the line \\(E'F'\\). Inversion preserves tangency, therefore the original line \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the original circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\nSince \\(\\ell\\) is precisely the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), the statement required in the problem holds.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
