ngle CB'D = \\angle CB'A = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]  \n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B'E'F'H'\\).**  \n   From (1)–(3) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   \\angle B'E'H' = \\angle B'F'H' = 90^{\\circ},\n   \\]  \n   i.e. the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'H'\\) has a pair of opposite right angles; therefore its four vertices lie on a common circle whose diameter is \\(B'H'\\).  \n\n6. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   In the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) the radius \\(B'H'\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at \\(B'\\); consequently the line \\(B'H'\\) is the tangent to \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).  \n\n   The image of the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is obtained as follows.  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(AD\\), i.e. to the line \\(CE'\\).  \n   A line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) therefore becomes a line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(CE'\\).  \n   Because \\(CE'\\) is perpendicular to \\(AD\\) and \\(B'H'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(AD\\) (by (3)), we have  \n   \\[\n   B'H' \\parallel CE'.\n   \\]  \n   Hence the image of the required line is exactly the tangent \\(B'H'\\) to \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).  \n\n7. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency: the pre‑image of a tangent line is a tangent line to the pre‑image of the circle.  \n   Therefore the pre‑image of the tangent \\(B'H'\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), and the pre‑image of the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\n   Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown.  \n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) the points on the line \\(MN\\) with the order \\(C,M,N,D\\),  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\),  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe must prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Inversion about \\((P,PA)\\)\n\nConsider the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(P\\) and power \\(PA^{2}\\).  \nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the points \\(A,C,D\\) lie on the inversion circle and are therefore fixed by \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\n*The images of the two given circles.*  \nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the fixed points \\(A\\) and respectively \\(C\\) or \\(D\\); consequently each of them is sent to the line joining the two fixed points:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=AC,\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=AD .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The image of the line \\(MN\\).*  \nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(P\\); therefore \\(\\mathcal I(MN)\\) is the circle \\((P,X)\\) where \\(X\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\).  \nDenote this circle by \\(\\omega\\).  The centre of \\(\\omega\\) is the midpoint of the segment \\(PX\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Images of the auxiliary points  \n\n*Image of \\(E\\).*  \n\\(E\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and on the line \\(AP\\).  \nUnder \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(AP\\) is sent to the line through the fixed point \\(A\\) that is orthogonal to the image of the circle \\(\\Omega\\), i.e. to the line through \\(A\\) orthogonal to \\(AC\\).  \nSince \\(A\\) is fixed, this image is precisely the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(AD\\); we denote it by \\(CE'\\).  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal I(E)=E'\\) is the intersection of the line \\(CE'\\) with the line \\(AC\\); in other words \\(E'\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(C\\) to \\(AD\\).\n\n*Image of \\(F\\).*  \nThe same reasoning with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives that \\(\\mathcal I(F)=F'\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(C\\) to \\(AC\\).\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{E' = \\text{foot from }C\\text{ to }AD},\\qquad \n\\boxed{F' = \\text{foot from }C\\text{ to }AC}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Image of \\(B\\).*  \n\\(B\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); after inversion it becomes the second intersection of the lines \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\), i.e. the point \\(A\\) itself, **unless** we take into account the fact that the inversion also sends the common external tangent at \\(A\\) to the line \\(AB\\).  \nA more convenient description is obtained by observing that the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(MN\\); consequently the image \\(B'\\) of \\(B\\) is the intersection of the two lines that are symmetric to the line \\(AB\\) with respect to the angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\).  \nEquivalently, \\(B'\\) is the unique point on the line through \\(C\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CB'D = \\angle CB'A = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe existence and uniqueness of such a point follow from elementary right‑angle geometry; we shall use (6) later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nIn \\(\\triangle PMN\\) the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\); let \\(X\\) be its foot.  \nBy definition of the orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes, hence \\(H\\) lies on the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) and on the line through \\(N\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\).\n\nUnder the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) the line \\(MN\\) becomes the circle \\(\\omega=(P,X)\\).  \nThe image of a point lying on an altitude is the reflection of the opposite vertex in the line containing that altitude.  \nConsequently the image \\(H'\\) of the orthocentre is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MN\\); equivalently, \\(H'\\) is the second intersection of the circle \\(\\omega\\) with the line \\(PH\\).  \n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{H' \\text{ is the reflection of }P\\text{ across }MN}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Right‑angle relations\n\nFrom the construction (5) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CE'D = \\angle CF'A = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(MN\\) is the external angle bisector of \\(\\angle CAD\\) (it is the line joining the centres of the two original circles), the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MN\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BH'H = \\angle CH'P = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nRelation (6) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CB'D = \\angle CB'A = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nCombining (8)–(10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B'E'H' = \\angle B'F'H' = 90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'H'\\) is cyclic  \n\nSince opposite angles of the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'H'\\) are right angles by (11), the four points lie on a common circle.  \nThe diameter of this circle is the segment \\(B'H'\\); indeed, any angle subtended by a diameter is a right angle.  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{B',E',F',H' \\text{ are concyclic with diameter } B'H'}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Tangency in the inverted picture  \n\nIn the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) the radius \\(B'H'\\) is perpendicular to the tangent at the point \\(B'\\).  \nTherefore the line \\(B'H'\\) is the tangent to \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\nNow consider the image of the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nThe line \\(AP\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the line \\(CE'\\) (the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(AD\\)).  \nConsequently a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is sent to a line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(CE'\\).  \n\nFrom (6) we have \\(\\angle (CE',\\,AD)=90^{\\circ}\\) and from (10) we have \\(\\angle (B'H',\\,AD)=90^{\\circ}\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nB'H' \\parallel CE'. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus the image of the required line is exactly the tangent \\(B'H'\\) to the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7.  Returning to the original configuration  \n\nInversion preserves tangency: the pre‑image of a tangent line is a tangent line to the pre‑image of the circle.  \nThe pre‑image of the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends each of the points \\(B,E,F\\) to \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \nThe pre‑image of the tangent \\(B'H'\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) (by construction of the image).  \n\nTherefore, in the original figure, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
