y in the inverted picture.**  \n   Equation (6) shows that the quadrilateral \\(B'E'F'H'\\) has a pair of opposite right angles; therefore the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(B'H'\\) is a diameter of the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\).  \n   In a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point, so the line \\(B'H'\\) is the tangent to \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n   The image of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is obtained as follows.  \n   The line \\(AP\\) is sent by \\(\\mathcal I\\) to the line through \\(C\\) perpendicular to \\(AD\\), i.e. to the line \\(CE'\\).  \n   Hence a line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) becomes a line through \\(H'\\) parallel to \\(CE'\\).  \n   By (4) the line \\(B'H'\\) is also perpendicular to \\(AD\\); consequently \\(B'H'\\parallel CE'\\).  \n   Thus the image of the required line is exactly the tangent \\(B'H'\\) to \\((B'E'F'H')\\) at \\(B'\\).\n\n7. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency.  \n   The pre‑image of the circle \\((B'E'F'H')\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) (because \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends each of the points \\(B,E,F\\) to \\(B',E',F'\\)).  \n   The pre‑image of the tangent \\(B'H'\\) is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n   Therefore, in the original figure, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
