so the two coincide.  \n   Since inversion preserves tangency, the pre‑image of the tangent \\(BH^{*}\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), and the pre‑image of the circle \\((B E^{*}F^{*})\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nThus the required tangency holds in the original figure. ∎\n\n\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, intersecting at the distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).  \nDenote by \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the order on the line being \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); let the line \\(AP\\) meet \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Preliminary observations  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the line of centres of the two intersecting circles; therefore it is perpendicular to the common chord \\(AB\\). Consequently \\(A\\notin MN\\) and \\(B\\notin MN\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)) and on \\(MN\\), we have \\(C\\neq A\\) and \\(D\\neq A\\).  \nThus the inversion centred at \\(A\\) that we shall use is well defined for all the points that appear.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Inversion at \\(A\\)\n\nPerform an inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(r\\).  \nBecause both circles pass through the centre of inversion, each of them is mapped to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\Omega^{*}= \\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\Gamma^{*}= \\ell_{\\Gamma},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)) is the image of \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \nSince the circles intersect at two distinct points, they are not tangent at \\(A\\); consequently \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\neq\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).\n\nThe points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) respectively, hence\n\n\\[\nC^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segments \\(PC\\) and \\(PD\\) are radii of the circles \\((ACD)\\) and are therefore equal to \\(PA\\).  Under inversion a point that is equidistant from \\(A\\) and a point on a circle through \\(A\\) is mapped to the foot of the perpendicular from the image of the centre to the image line.  Hence\n\n\\[\nC^{*}\\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }P^{*}\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Omega},\n\\]\n\\[\nD^{*}\\text{ is the foot of the perpendicular from }P^{*}\\text{ to }\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant as a set; its image is the same line, which we denote by \\(AP^{*}\\).  \nSince \\(E\\) (resp. \\(F\\)) is the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)), the points \\(E^{*},F^{*}\\) are the reflections of \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP^{*}\\):\n\n\\[\nE^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ across }AP^{*},\\qquad\nF^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ across }AP^{*}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection of the two circles is \\(B\\); because inversion sends each circle to a line, the image of \\(B\\) is the intersection of those lines:\n\n\\[\nB^{*}= \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}=B .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nSince \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\) and \\(X^{*}\\); the points \\(A\\) and \\(X^{*}\\) are antipodal on \\(\\omega\\) because \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=r^{2}\\) and \\(AX\\) is a radius of the inversion.\n\nThe altitude \\(PX\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); after inversion the line \\(PX\\) becomes the circle \\(\\omega\\) and the point \\(P\\) becomes \\(P^{*}\\).  Consequently the image of the altitude is the diameter \\(AP^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \n\nThe orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is the intersection of the three altitudes; under inversion the three altitudes become the three circles through \\(A\\) having as diameters the three lines \\(AP^{*},\\,AQ^{*},\\,AR^{*}\\) (where \\(Q,R\\) are the feet from \\(M,N\\) respectively).  Their common point is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\); denote this point by \\(H^{*}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }AX^{*}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AX^{*}\\) is perpendicular to \\(AP^{*}\\), the reflection property gives\n\n\\[\nAH^{*}\\parallel AP^{*}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Right‑angle relations in the inverted configuration  \n\nFrom (1) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C^{*}AP^{*}= \\angle D^{*}AP^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2) the reflections yield  \n\n\\[\n\\angle E^{*}C^{*}A =\\angle F^{*}D^{*}A =90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are the images of the two circles, the common chord \\(AB\\) is mapped to their intersection point \\(B\\); the chord is perpendicular to the line of centres, therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\angle C^{*}B D^{*}= \\angle C^{*}B A = \\angle D^{*}B A =90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFinally, using (4) and the fact that \\(AX^{*}\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(P^{*}H^{*}\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BH^{*}P^{*}= \\angle BP^{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Cyclicity of \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\) and the tangent  \n\nFrom (7) we know that \\(\\angle E^{*}B C^{*}=90^{\\circ}\\) (because \\(C^{*},B\\) lie on \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\)) and similarly \\(\\angle F^{*}B D^{*}=90^{\\circ}\\).  \nTogether with (9) we see that the quadrilateral \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\) has right angles at the opposite vertices \\(E^{*}\\) and \\(H^{*}\\); therefore the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(BH^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.  \n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point; consequently the line \\(BH^{*}\\) is tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Transfer back to the original figure  \n\nBy (5) the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) coincides with the line \\(BH^{*}\\).  \nSince inversion preserves parallelism through the centre and preserves tangency, the pre‑image of the tangent \\(BH^{*}\\) is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), and the pre‑image of the circle \\((B E^{*}F^{*})\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n\nHence, in the original configuration, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n∎"
