rough \\(A\\); therefore under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it is sent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A\\).  \n   The points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are the centres of the original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\); they are fixed by the inversion because \\(AM\\cdot AM=R^{2}=AN\\cdot AN\\) (the radii of the two circles are equal to the distances from \\(A\\) to the centres).  \n   Consequently the circle \\(\\omega\\) is the circumcircle of the triangle \\(A X^{*} M\\) (or \\(A X^{*} N\\)); in particular \\(A\\) and \\(X^{*}\\) are antipodal points on \\(\\omega\\).  \n\n   Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\), the image \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(M X^{*}\\).  \n   Because \\(M\\) lies on the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the centre of \\(\\Omega\\) lies on the perpendicular to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) through \\(A\\)), the line \\(M X^{*}\\) coincides with the line \\(A X^{*}\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }A X^{*}. \\tag{6}\n   \\]  \n\n   As \\(A X^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), it is perpendicular to the chord \\(P^{*}H^{*}\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle B P^{*} H^{*}= \\angle B H^{*} P^{*}=90^{\\circ}, \\tag{7}\n   \\]  \n\n   because \\(B\\) is the intersection \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and each of the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) is perpendicular to the corresponding segment \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) or \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\) (by (2)).\n\n5. **Right‑angle relations in the inverted picture.**  \n   From (2) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C^{*}A P^{*}= \\angle D^{*}A P^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{8}\n   \\]  \n\n   From (5) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle E^{*}C^{*}A= \\angle F^{*}D^{*}A=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{9}\n   \\]  \n\n   The point \\(B\\) is the intersection of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle C^{*}B D^{*}= \\angle C^{*}B A= \\angle D^{*}B A=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{10}\n   \\]  \n\n   Together with (7) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle B H^{*} P^{*}= \\angle B P^{*} H^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Cyclicity and tangency after inversion.**  \n   From (9)–(11) we see that the quadrilateral \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\) has opposite right angles:\n   \\[\n   \\angle B E^{*} H^{*}= \\angle B F^{*} H^{*}=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]  \n   Hence the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(B H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.  \n   In a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point; therefore the line \\(B H^{*}\\) is tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E^{*} F^{*}\\).\n\n   Moreover, by (8) the line \\(A P^{*}\\) is perpendicular to both \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(A P^{*}\\) is thus also perpendicular to those two lines, i.e. it coincides with the tangent \\(B H^{*}\\).\n\n7. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   The inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) sends the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(A P^{*}\\); we have just proved that the latter line is the tangent at \\(B\\) to the circle \\((B E^{*} F^{*})\\).  \n   Because inversion preserves tangency, the pre‑image of this tangent is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), and the pre‑image of the circle \\((B E^{*} F^{*})\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).  \n\n   Hence, in the original figure, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
