{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the quadrilateral \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\) is cyclic with diameter \\(BH^{*}\\).\n\n4. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   Because \\(BH^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) coincides with \\(BH^{*}\\) and is therefore tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E^{*} F^{*}\\).\n\n5. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency and sends the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\).  \n   Hence the original line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(r_{1}=MC<r_{2}=ND\\) and \\(A,B\\) their intersection points.  \nDenote by \\(C\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the point nearer to \\(M\\)) and by \\(D\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the point nearer to \\(N\\)).  \nLet \\(P\\) be the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\); let \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nFinally, let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and radius  \n\n\\[\nR:=AM=AN .\n\\]\n\nBecause the two circles pass through the centre of inversion, each of them is mapped to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AM\\cdot AM=R^{2}=AN\\cdot AN\\), the points \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) satisfy the defining relation of an inversion and therefore are **fixed**:\n\n\\[\nM^{*}=M,\\qquad N^{*}=N .\n\\]\n\nDenote by a superscript \\(*\\) the image of a point under \\(\\mathcal I\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of the basic points\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD .\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(C^{*}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(AP^{*}\\).  \nMoreover \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nC^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AC^{*}=AP^{*}\\) and the segment \\(AP^{*}\\) is symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP^{*}\\) itself, the triangle \\(AP^{*}C^{*}\\) is isosceles with vertex at \\(A\\) and base \\(C^{*}\\). Consequently the altitude from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) meets \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) at \\(C^{*}\\); i.e.\n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant: \\(\\mathcal I(AP)=AP\\).  \nThe second intersection of this line with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\); after inversion we obtain the second intersection of \\(AP^{*}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\). Because \\(C^{*}\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\), the second intersection is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP^{*}\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nE^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously,\n\n\\[\nF^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2)–(3) we obtain the right angles\n\n\\[\n\\angle E^{*}C^{*}A=\\angle F^{*}D^{*}A=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).  \n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); under the inversion it is sent to a circle \\(\\omega\\) that passes through \\(A\\) and the images of \\(M\\) and \\(N\\), i.e. through \\(M\\) and \\(N\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) are fixed, \\(\\omega\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AMN\\).  \nThe points \\(X\\) and \\(X^{*}\\) are antipodal on \\(\\omega\\) because \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\) and \\(AM\\cdot AM=R^{2}=AN\\cdot AN\\); consequently the diameter of \\(\\omega\\) is \\(AX^{*}\\).\n\nReflecting \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\) and applying the inversion gives that \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the image of \\(MX\\).  \nSince \\(M\\) is fixed and lies on the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the perpendicular from \\(M\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) passes through \\(A\\) and \\(P^{*}\\)), the image of \\(MX\\) is exactly the line \\(AX^{*}\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }AX^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nAX^{*}\\perp P^{*}H^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthogonalities involving \\(B\\)\n\nThe point \\(B\\) is the second intersection of the two circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); after inversion it is the intersection of the two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nFrom (1) we have \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\n\\angle BP^{*}C^{*}= \\angle BP^{*}D^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Cyclic quadrilateral \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\)\n\nUsing (4), (6) and (7) we compute the following angles:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle BE^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle BE^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*}\n    =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ},\\\\\n\\angle BF^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle BF^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}F^{*}\n    =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B\\,E^{*}\\,F^{*}\\,H^{*}\\) are right angles; consequently the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(BH^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  \nSince \\(BH^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is parallel to the radius \\(AB\\) (which coincides with the line \\(AP^{*}\\)) is exactly the line \\(BH^{*}\\).  \nHence the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) is tangent at \\(B\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E^{*} F^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nThe inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) maps\n\n* the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) onto the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\);\n* the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\) onto the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E^{*} F^{*}\\).\n\nBecause inversion preserves tangency, the pre‑image of the tangent just proved is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nTherefore, in the original figure, this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B E F\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
