he original circles are reflected in the line \\(AP\\) after inversion; therefore*\n   \\[\n   E^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ in }AP,\\qquad\n   F^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ in }AP .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   By definition \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).  \n\n   The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); under the inversion it becomes the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  \n   The point \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\), hence \\(X^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\) (because \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\)).  \n   Consequently the image of the line \\(MX\\) is the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \n\n   Reflecting \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\) and then inverting gives that **\\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\)**.  \n   Since \\(AX^{*}\\) is the line through the two points \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) (they are the ends of the same diameter of \\(\\omega\\)), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in the line }M^{*}N^{*}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}\\).\n\n4. **Right‑angle relations.**  \n   From the construction we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n   &AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}\\qquad\\text{(by (1))},\\\\\n   &M^{*}N^{*}\\perp P^{*}H^{*}\\qquad\\text{(by (2))},\\\\\n   &B^{*}C^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad B^{*}D^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}\\qquad\\text{(because }B^{*}=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\text{)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}= \\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   i.e. the quadrilateral