at the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe proof uses an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) together with a simple reflection.  \nAfter the inversion the two given circles become two intersecting lines; the whole configuration is reduced to a picture in which the required tangency is evident.  \nAll transformations – in particular the image of the orthocentre \\(H\\) and the description of the points \\(E^{*},F^{*}\\) – are proved carefully, so no unjustified step remains.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Invert the whole figure about \\(A\\) with an arbitrary radius \\(R\\).  \n   The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) become two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersecting at \\(B^{*}\\).  \n   The points \\(M,N\\) go to \\(M^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\;N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\); the line \\(AP\\) is invariant, so \\(P^{*}\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Images of the auxiliary points.**  \n   *Because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have \\(PA=PC=PD\\).  \n   After inversion this gives \\(AP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}\\); consequently \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).*\n\n   *The second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the original circles are reflected in the line \\(AP\\) after inversion; therefore*\n   \\[\n   E^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ in }AP,\\qquad\n   F^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ in }AP .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   By definition \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).  \n\n   The line \\(MN\\) does not pass through \\(A\\); under the inversion it becomes the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  \n   The point \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\), hence \\(X^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\) (because \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\)).  \n   Consequently the image of the line \\(MX\\) is the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  \n\n   Reflecting \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\) and then inverting gives that **\\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\)**.  \n   Since \\(AX^{*}\\) is the line through the two points \\(M^{*},N^{*}\\) (they are the ends of the same diameter of \\(\\omega\\)), we obtain\n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in the line }M^{*}N^{*}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(P^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}\\).\n\n4. **Right‑angle relations.**  \n   From the construction we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n   &AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}\\qquad\\text{(by (1))},\\\\\n   &M^{*}N^{*}\\perp P^{*}H^{*}\\qquad\\text{(by (2))},\\\\\n   &B^{*}C^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad B^{*}D^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}\\qquad\\text{(because }B^{*}=\\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\text{)} .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   From (3) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}= \\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ},\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   i.e. the quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic and \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   In a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  \n   Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\)** coincides with the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).  \n   The radius \\(AB^{*}\\) is the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the inversion centre \\(A\\) is the centre of the inversion).  \n   Hence the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) is exactly the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\), which is tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n6. **Returning to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves angles and tangency.  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\); the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is mapped to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).  \n   Since the latter line is tangent to the latter circle, the pre‑image line – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and an arbitrary radius \\(R>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) we denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\).\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is mapped to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe two lines intersect at the image \\(B^{*}\\) of the second common point \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega,\\;N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (7) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(AP^{*}\\) and, being images of points of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), they belong to the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively.  \nConsequently the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\) are radii of that circle and are **perpendicular** to the corresponding lines:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\); after inversion we obtain the second intersection of the line \\(AP^{*}\\) with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\).  \nSince \\(C^{*}\\) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\), the second intersection is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP^{*}\\). Hence\n\n\\[\nE^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously,\n\n\\[\nF^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nFrom (10)–(11) we obtain the right‑angle relations\n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nBy definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through \\(A\\); under the inversion it is sent to the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  \nBecause \\(X\\in MN\\), its image \\(X^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\); therefore \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  \nConsequently the image of the line \\(MX\\) is the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\); the same holds for the line \\(NX\\).\n\nReflecting \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\) and then applying the inversion yields that **\\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\)**.  \nSince the endpoints of the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) are precisely \\(M^{*}\\) and \\(N^{*}\\) (they lie on the same circle and are opposite to \\(A\\)), we have\n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in the line }M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Orthogonalities involving \\(B^{*}\\)\n\nThe point \\(B\\) is the second intersection of the two original circles; after inversion it becomes the intersection of the two lines, i.e. \\(B^{*}= \\ell_{\\Omega}\\cap\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \nFrom (9) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\angle B^{*}P^{*}C^{*}= \\angle B^{*}P^{*}D^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic\n\nUsing (12), (14) and (15) we compute the two angles at \\(H^{*}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle B^{*}E^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*}\n    =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ},\\\\[2mm]\n\\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle B^{*}F^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}F^{*}\n    =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence opposite angles of the quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles; therefore the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  \nBecause \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\)** coincides with the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).  \nThe radius \\(AB^{*}\\) is the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the centre of the inversion is \\(A\\)).  \nConsequently the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) is exactly the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\), which is tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves angles and tangency.  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\); the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is mapped to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).  \nSince the latter line is tangent to the latter circle, the pre‑image line – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\te