t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe proof proceeds by an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) of the two given circles, which transforms the circles into intersecting lines. In the inverted picture the required tangency becomes obvious: the image of the orthocenter \\(H\\) is the reflection of the image of \\(P\\) in the line joining the images of the centres, and the images of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the reflections of the feet of the perpendiculars from that point to the two lines. Consequently the quadrilateral formed by the images of \\(B,E,F,H\\) is cyclic with a diameter through the image of \\(B\\); the line through the image of \\(H\\) parallel to the image of \\(AP\\) is exactly this diameter and therefore tangent to the circumcircle of the image of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). Inverting back yields the desired tangency in the original configuration.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Invert the whole figure about \\(A\\) with arbitrary radius \\(R\\).  \n   - The circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) become two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersecting at \\(B^{*}\\).  \n   - The centres