h \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\); the image \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  \n   *The line \\(MX\\) (and likewise \\(NX\\)) is sent to the circle through \\(M^{*},X^{*},A\\). Because \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), this circle is exactly the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\); consequently its image is the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).  \n   *Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\), after inversion \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the image of \\(MX\\), i.e. in the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\). Thus  \n     \\[\n     H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad P^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Right‑angle relations.**  \n   From the constructions we have  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n   &AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*},\\\\\n   &M^{*}N^{*}\\perp P^{*}H^{*},\\\\\n   &B^{*}C^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad B^{*}D^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\).**  \n   Using (1) we compute  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}\n      &=\\angle B^{*}E^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*}\n       =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ},\\\\\n   \\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\n      &=\\angle B^{*}F^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}F^{*}\n       =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Hence opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles; the four points are concyclic and \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n6. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   In a circle the radius through a point is perpendicular to the tangent at that point. Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\) coincides with \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\); therefore it is tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n7. **Preserv