ss through the centre of inversion; under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it is sent to the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through the three points \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  Because \\(X\\in MN\\), its image \\(X^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\); hence \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).\n\nConsider the line \\(MX\\).  Its image is the circle through the three points \\(M^{*},X^{*},A\\).  Since \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), the points \\(M^{*},N^{*},X^{*}\\) are collinear: indeed, the chord \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\) is perpendicular to the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\), and the only chord of a circle perpendicular to a given diameter passes through the antipode of the centre of the diameter, i.e. through \\(X^{*}\\).  Consequently the circle through \\(M^{*},X^{*},A\\) coincides with the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\); its image is the straight line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).  The same reasoning shows that the image of \\(NX\\) is also the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\), after inversion we obtain that \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the image of \\(MX\\), i.e. in the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Orthogonalities involving \\(B^{*}\\)\n\nBecause \\(B^{*}\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), from (6) we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle B^{*}P^{*}C^{*}= \\angle B^{*}P^{*}D^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. The quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic\n\nUsing (9), (10) and (11) we compute the two angles at \\(H^{*}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle B^{*}E^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*} \\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{90^{\\circ}}_{\\text{by