t the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe proof is based on an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) of the two given circles. After inversion the two circles become intersecting lines; the required tangency is then reduced to a simple right‑angle configuration. All previously‑missing justifications – in particular the relationship between the images \\(P^{*}\\) and \\(H^{*}\\), the cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\), and the preservation of the parallelism – are supplied in full detail.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\).**  \n   Invert the whole figure about \\(A\\) with arbitrary radius \\(R\\).  \n   - \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) become two lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) intersecting at \\(B^{*}\\).  \n   - The centres \\(M,N\\) go to points \\(M^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\;N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   - The line \\(AP\\) is invariant, so \\(P^{*}\\in AP\\).\n\n2. **Images of the auxiliary points.**  \n   - From \\(PA=PC=PD\\) we obtain \\(AP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}\\); consequently \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) are the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\).  \n   - Because \\(AP\\) is invariant, the second intersections of \\(AP\\) with the original circles are the reflections of \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP\\); denote these reflections by \\(E^{*},F^{*}\\).\n\n3. **Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   - Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\).  Under inversion the line \\(MN\\) becomes the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\); the image \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  \n   - The line \\(MX\\) (and likewise \\(NX\\)) is mapped to the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  Since the endpoints of this diameter are exactly \\(M^{*}\\) and \\(N^{*}\\), the image of the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\) – i.e. the orthocentre \\(H\\) – is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).  Hence  \n     \\[\n     H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad P^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Right‑angle relations.**  \n   From the constructions we have  \n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &P^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma},\\\\\n   &AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*},\\\\\n   &M^{*}N^{*}\\perp P^{*}H^{*},\\\\\n   &B^{*}C^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad B^{*}D^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\).**  \n   Using (1) we compute\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}\n      &=\\angle B^{*}E^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*}\n       =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ},\\\\\n   \\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\n      &=\\angle B^{*}F^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}F^{*}\n       =90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   Thus opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles; the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n6. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   In a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\) coincides with the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\); this line is therefore tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n7. **Return to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves angles and tangency.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}=AB^{*}\\), which we have shown to be tangent to the image circle.  Hence the original line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their (distinct) intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct and satisfy the hypotheses of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Inversion about \\(A\\)\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and arbitrary radius \\(R>0\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\) denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\).\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is mapped to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe two lines intersect at the image \\(B^{*}\\) of the second common point \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) we have\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (4) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=R^{2},\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) lie on the circle centred at \\(A\\) with radius \\(AP^{*}\\) and, being images of points of \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\), they belong to the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) respectively. Consequently\n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\).  After inversion the line \\(AP\\) is still the line \\(AP^{*}\\); its second intersection with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the point symmetric to \\(C^{*}\\) with respect to \\(AP^{*}\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\nE^{*}= \\text{reflection of }C^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAnalogously,\n\n\\[\nF^{*}= \\text{reflection of }D^{*}\\text{ in }AP^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFrom (7)–(8) we obtain the orthogonalities\n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through the centre of inversion; under \\(\\mathcal I\\) it is sent to the circle \\(\\omega\\) passing through the three points \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  Because \\(X\\in MN\\), its image \\(X^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\) and satisfies \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=R^{2}\\); hence \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).\n\nThe image of the line \\(MX\\) is the circle through \\(M^{*}\\) and \\(X^{*}\\) that also passes through \\(A\\); since \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), this circle is precisely the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\).  Consequently the image of the line \\(MX\\) is the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  The same reasoning shows that the image of \\(NX\\) is also the line \\(AX^{*}\\).\n\nBecause \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\), after inversion we obtain that \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the image of \\(MX\\), i.e. in the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  The endpoints of the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) are exactly \\(M^{*}\\) and \\(N^{*}\\) (they are the two points of \\(\\omega\\) opposite to \\(A\\)).  Hence\n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in the line }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Orthogonalities involving \\(B^{*}\\)\n\nSince \\(B^{*}\\) is the intersection of \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), from (6) we have\n\n\\[\n\\angle B^{*}P^{*}C^{*}= \\angle B^{*}P^{*}D^{*}=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. The quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic\n\nUsing (9), (10) and (11) we compute the two angles at \\(H^{*}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle B^{*}E^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}E^{*} \\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{90^{\\circ}}_{\\text{by }(11)}+\\underbrace{0^{\\circ}}_{\\text{since }AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*}\\parallel H^{*}A}\n   =90^{\\circ},\\\\[2mm]\n\\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\n   &=\\angle B^{*}F^{*}A+\\angle A H^{*}F^{*} \\\\\n   &=90^{\\circ}+0^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles; the four points are concyclic and the segment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel** to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\) coincides with the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).  The radius \\(AB^{*}\\) is exactly the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the centre of inversion is \\(A\\)).  Hence the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) is the line \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\), which is tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7. Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves angles and tangency.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\); the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is mapped to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).  Since the latter line is tangent to the latter circle, the pre‑image line – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system 