is mapped to a line:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\Omega)=\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad \\mathcal I(\\Gamma)=\\ell_{\\Gamma}.\n\\]\n\nThe two lines intersect at the image \\(B^{*}\\) of the second common point \\(B\\).\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (3) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nBecause the power \\(k\\) equals \\(PA^{2}\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is equidistant from \\(P^{*},C^{*},D^{*}\\); consequently \\(A\\) lies on the perpendicular bisectors of the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp AC^{*},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp AD^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), (5) tells us that \\(C^{*}\\) (resp. \\(D^{*}\\)) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\).  After inversion the line \\(AP\\) is still the line \\(AP^{*}\\); its second intersection with \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP^{*}\\).  Denote this reflection by \\(E^{*}\\).  Similarly, the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(D^{*}\\) in \\(AP^{*}\\); denote it by \\(F^{*}\\).  Therefore  \n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Image of the 