e original circles are the reflections of \\(C^{*},D^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP\\); denote them by \\(E^{*},F^{*}\\).  Thus  \n   \\[\n   AE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\).**  \n   Let \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\).  Under the inversion the line \\(MN\\) (which does not pass through \\(A\\)) becomes the circle \\(\\omega\\) through \\(A,M^{*},N^{*}\\).  Because \\(X\\in MN\\), its image \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\).  The line \\(MX\\) (and likewise \\(NX\\)) is mapped to the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\), the image \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\); but the latter line is exactly \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).  Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   H^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad P^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\).**  \n   From the orthogonalities obtained in steps 2 and 3 we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle B^{*}E^{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ},\\qquad \n   \\angle B^{*}F^{*}H^{*}=90^{\\circ}.\n   \\]  \n\n   Thus opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles; the four points are concyclic and \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter of its circumcircle.\n\n5. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   In a circle the radius through a point is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel** to the radius \\(AB^{*}\\) coincides with the diameter \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\); consequently it is tangent at \\(B^{*}\\) to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).  The radius \\(AB^{*}\\) is precisely the line \\(AP^{*}\\) (the centre of inversion is \\(A\\)), so the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP^{*}\\) is tangent to that circle.\n\n6. **Return to the original configuration.**  \n   In