\\(P\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (4) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nUsing the value of \\(k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp AC^{*},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp AD^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), (6) tells us that \\(C^{*}\\) (resp. \\(D^{*}\\)) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\).  After inversion the line \\(AP\\) is still the line \\(AP\\); its second intersection with the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP\\).  Denote this reflection by \\(E^{*}\\).  Similarly, the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(D^{*}\\) in \\(AP\\); denote it by \\(F^{*}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).\n\nBecause the line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle; denote it by  \n\n\\[\n\\omega=\\mathcal I(MN).\n\\]\n\nThe circle \\(\\omega\\) passes through the images of the three points \\(A,M,N\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\omega\\text{ is the circle through }A,\\;M^{*},\\;N^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\); let \\(X^{*}\\) be its image.  From the definition of inversion,\n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AX\\) is the altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\), we have \\(AX\\perp MN\\).  Inversion sends a line perpendicular to the pre‑image of a circle into a diameter of that circle; consequently \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\).  In particular, \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAX^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the line \\(MX\\).  It does not pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image is a circle passing through \\(A,M^{*},X^{*}\\).  Because \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), the circle \\((A M^{*} X^{*})\\) coincides with the **diameter** \\(AX^{*}\\) of \\(\\omega\\).  Hence the image of the line \\(MX\\) is precisely the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  The same reasoning shows that the image of the line \\(NX\\) is also the line \\(AX^{*}\\).\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\), after inversion the point \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  Using (9) we may replace \\(AX^{*}\\) by the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Right angles at \\(H^{*}\\)\n\nFrom (1) we know that the parallel lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) all have direction \\(AB\\).  Using (6)–(10) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},AB)\n   &=\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}H^{*})+\\angle(P^{*}H^{*},AB)  \\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}C^{*})}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(7)}+\n      \\underbrace{\\angle(P^{*}C^{*},AB)}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(6)\\text{ and }(1)}\\\\\n   &=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAn identical computation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(F^{*}H^{*},AB)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (i.e. the line