                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and hypotheses  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\);  \n  \\(\\displaystyle r_{\\Omega}<r_{\\Gamma}\\).\n* The circles intersect at the two distinct points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n* The line \\(MN\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(C\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(D\\) with the order  \n  \\(C\\!-\\!M\\!-\\!N\\!-\\!D\\) on the line.\n* \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).\n* The line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\neq A\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\neq A\\).\n* \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct and the configuration is non‑degenerate (in particular\n\\(P\\notin MN\\); otherwise \\(\\triangle PMN\\) would be collinear and the orthocentre\nwould not be defined).\n\nWe have to prove:\n\n> **Claim.**  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the\n> circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inversion centred at \\(A\\)\n\nLet \\(\\mathcal I\\) be the inversion with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk:=AP\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) we denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Images of the two given circles  \n\nBoth \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, therefore each\nis sent to a line.  Because  \n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AB^{*}=k=AP\\cdot AB,\n\\]\n\nwe obtain \\(AB^{*}=\\infty\\); i.e. the image of the line \\(AB\\) is the point at\ninfinity in the direction of \\(AB\\).  Consequently the two image lines are\nparallel and have direction \\(AB\\).  We write  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}:=\\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Gamma}:=\\mathcal I(\\Gamma),\\qquad \n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel AB . \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, hence it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (2.4) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nUsing the value of \\(k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp AC^{*},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp AD^{*}. \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), (2.6) tells us that\n\\(C^{*}\\) (resp. \\(D^{*}\\)) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to\n\\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\).  After inversion\nthe line \\(AP\\) is still the line \\(AP\\); its second intersection with the line\n\\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP\\).  Denote this\nreflection by \\(E^{*}\\).  Similarly, the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the\nreflection of \\(D^{*}\\) in \\(AP\\); denote it by \\(F^{*}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{2.7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  The altitude from \\(P\\) to \\(MN\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\); i.e.  \n\n\\[\nPX\\perp MN,\\qquad X\\in MN . \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the orthocentre is the reflection of a vertex in the opposite side,\n\\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Image of the line \\(MN\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) does not pass through the centre \\(A\\); therefore its image under\n\\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle, which we denote by  \n\n\\[\n\\omega:=\\mathcal I(MN).\n\\]\n\nSince inversion sends a line to a circle passing through the centre of inversion,\n\\(\\omega\\) passes through \\(A\\) and through the images of the two points \\(M\\) and\n\\(N\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\omega\\text{ is the circle through }A,\\;M^{*},\\;N^{*}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Image of the foot \\(X\\)\n\nFrom (3.1) we have \\(AX\\cdot AX^{*}=k\\).  Moreover \\(AX\\perp MN\\); a standard\nproperty of inversion states:\n\n> *If a line \\(\\ell\\) does not pass through the centre of inversion and a point\n> \\(Y\\) on \\(\\ell\\) satisfies \\(AY\\perp\\ell\\), then the image \\(Y^{*}\\) is the\n> antipode of \\(A\\) on the circle \\(\\mathcal I(\\ell)\\).*\n\nApplying this to \\(\\ell=MN\\) we obtain that \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on\n\\(\\omega\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\nAX^{*}\\text{ is a diameter of }\\omega\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nAX^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.4  Image of the line \\(MX\\)\n\nThe line \\(MX\\) also does not pass through \\(A\\); its image is the circle\n\\(\\mathcal I(MX)\\) passing through \\(A,M^{*},X^{*}\\).  Because \\(AX^{*}\\) is a\ndiameter of \\(\\omega\\) and \\(M^{*}\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\), the three points\n\\(A,M^{*},X^{*}\\) are collinear on the diameter \\(AX^{*}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(MX)=AX^{*}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nThe same argument shows \\(\\mathcal I(NX)=AX^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.5  Image of the orthocentre  \n\nSince \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\), after inversion the\npoint \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the image of \\(MX\\), i.e. in the\nline \\(AX^{*}\\).  Using (3.3) we may replace \\(AX^{*}\\) by the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\);\ntherefore  \n\n\\[\nH^{*}\\text{ is the reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Right angles at \\(H^{*}\\)\n\nFrom (2.1) we have \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel M^{*}N^{*}\\parallel AB\\).\nUsing (2.6), (2.7) and (3.5) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},AB)\n   &=\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}H^{*})+\\angle(P^{*}H^{*},AB)\\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}C^{*})}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(2.7)}\n     +\\underbrace{\\angle(P^{*}C^{*},AB)}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(2.6)\\text{ and }(2.1)}\\\\\n   &=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nExactly the same computation with \\(F^{*}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(F^{*}H^{*},AB)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (hence also parallel to\n\\(AP\\), because both pass through the centre \\(A\\)) is perpendicular to both\n\\(E^{*}H^{*}\\) and \\(F^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Cyclic quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\)\n\nRecall that \\(B^{*}\\) is the point at infinity in the direction of \\(AB\\); in the\ninverted picture this means that the line \\(AB\\) is parallel to the line\n\\(B^{*}X\\) for any finite point \\(X\\).  Consequently the angles\n\n\\[\n\\angle(E^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\angle(F^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\n\\]\n\nare exactly the angles computed in (4.1), i.e. both are right angles.\nHence the quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic and its diameter is the\nsegment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the\ntangent at that point.  Because \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through\n\\(H^{*}\\) parallel to the radius \\(AB\\) coincides with the diameter itself.\nTherefore this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\nSince \\(AB\\) and \\(AP\\) have the same direction (both pass through the centre\n\\(A\\)), the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the tangent\ndescribed above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves angles and tangency, and it fixes every line that passes\nthrough its centre \\(A\\).  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is mapped to the\ncircumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\); the line through \\(H\\) parallel\nto \\(AP\\) is mapped to the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence the\npre‑image of the tangent found in Section 6 is precisely the line through \\(H\\)\nparallel to \\(AP\\).\n\nThus the required tangency holds in the original figure.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
