at the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete, rigorous solution.  \nThe proof is based on an inversion centred at the common point \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP\\cdot AB\\).  \nThe inversion sends the two given circles to two parallel lines; the images of the auxiliary points acquire a very simple orthogonal description.  \nThe only delicate point is the description of the image \\(H^{*}\\) of the orthocentre \\(H\\).  \nI give a detailed justification that \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) (which is parallel to the direction \\(AB\\)).  \nWith this description the right‑angle relations at \\(H^{*}\\) become immediate, yielding the cyclic quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) with diameter \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).  \nSince \\(B^{*}\\) is the point at infinity in the direction \\(AB\\), the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the diameter \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) and is therefore tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).  \nInversion preserves tangency and fixes every line through its centre \\(A\\); consequently the pre‑image of that tangent is the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }