\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the two given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their distinct intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct and satisfy the hypotheses of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1. Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP\\cdot AB\\)\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AP\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\).\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is mapped to a line.  \nSince  \n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AB^{*}=k=AP\\cdot AB,\n\\]\n\nwe have \\(AB^{*}= \\infty\\); consequently the two image lines are **parallel**.  \nDenote them by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nThe direction of these parallel lines is the image of the line \\(AB\\); because \\(AB\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\), its image is the same line. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel AB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nFrom the definition of 