 \n   The image of the line \\(MX\\) (and also \\(NX\\)) is the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  \n   Since \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in \\(MX\\), its image \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in \\(AX^{*}\\); using the fact that \\(AX^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}\\) we obtain that \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\).\n\n4. **Right angles at \\(H^{*}\\).**  \n   From the perpendicular relations above and the fact that \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel M^{*}N^{*}\\parallel AB\\), we get  \n   \\(\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},AB)=\\angle(F^{*}H^{*},AB)=90^{\\circ}\\).\n\n5. **Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\).**  \n   The two opposite angles of quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) are right angles, hence it is cyclic and \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter.\n\n6. **Tangency in the inverted picture.**  \n   The line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (which is the same direction as \\(AP\\)) coincides with the diameter \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\); therefore it is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n7. **Return to the original configuration.**  \n   Inversion preserves tangency and fixes every line through its centre \\(A\\).  Hence the pre‑image of the tangent is exactly the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), proving the required statement.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively,  \n* \\(A,B\\) their distinct intersection points,  \n* \\(C\\) (resp. \\(D\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)),  \n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nAll points are distinct and satisfy the hypotheses of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP\\cdot AB\\)\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AP\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\) denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\).\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is mapped to a line.  \nSince  \n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AB^{*}=k=AP\\cdot AB,\n\\]\n\nwe have \\(AB^{*}= \\infty\\); consequently the two image lines are **parallel**.  \nDenote them by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nThe direction of these parallel lines is the image of the line \\(AB\\); because \\(AB\\) passes through the centre \\(A\\), its image is the same line. Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel AB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\),\n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nFrom the definition of \\(P\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (4) by the corresponding inverse distance gives  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nUsing the value of \\(k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp AC^{*},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp AD^{*}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), (6) tells us that \\(C^{*}\\) (resp. \\(D^{*}\\)) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of the line