cisely the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  The same reasoning shows that the image of the line \\(NX\\) is also the line \\(AX^{*}\\).\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\), after inversion the point \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  Using (9) we may replace \\(AX^{*}\\) by the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Right angles at \\(H^{*}\\)\n\nFrom (1) we know that the parallel lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) all have direction \\(AB\\).  Using (6)–(10) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},AB)\n   &=\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}H^{*})+\\angle(P^{*}H^{*},AB)  \\\\\n   &=\\underbrace{\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}C^{*})}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(7)}+\n      \\underbrace{\\angle(P^{*}C^{*},AB)}_{=90^{\\circ}\\text{ by }(6)\\text{ and }(1)}\\\\\n   &=90^{\\circ}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAn identical computation gives  \n\n\\[\n\\angle(F^{*}H^{*},AB)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (i.e. the line \\(H^{*}B^{*}\\)) is perpendicular to both \\(E^{*}H^{*}\\) and \\(F^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\)\n\nBecause the two opposite angles \\(\\angle(E^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\\) and \\(\\angle(F^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\\) are right angles by (11), the quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic and its diameter is the segment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Since \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel**