 Inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP\\cdot AB\\)\n\nPerform the inversion \\(\\mathcal I\\) with centre \\(A\\) and power  \n\n\\[\nk=AP\\cdot AB .\n\\]\n\nFor any point \\(X\\neq A\\) denote its image by \\(X^{*}\\); thus  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=k . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) pass through the centre of inversion, each is mapped to a line.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}= \\mathcal I(\\Omega),\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}= \\mathcal I(\\Gamma).\n\\]\n\nApplying (1) to the common point \\(B\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nAB\\cdot AB^{*}=k=AP\\cdot AB\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad AB^{*}=AP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nHence \\(B^{*}\\) is a **finite** point on the line \\(AB\\) at distance \\(AP\\) from \\(A\\).  \nConsequently the two image lines intersect at the point \\(B^{*}\\); they are not parallel.\n\nSince the circles intersect at \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), the images \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) are the lines through \\(B^{*}\\) that are the images of the circles, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\; \\text{and}\\; \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\; \\text{both pass through }B^{*}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom (1) applied to \\(M\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(N\\in\\Gamma\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nM^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega},\\qquad N^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) passes through the centre of inversion, therefore it is invariant:\n\n\\[\nP^{*}\\in AP . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2. Images of \\(C,D,E,F\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nMultiplying each equality in (6) by the corresponding inverse distance and using (1) yields  \n\n\\[\nAP\\cdot AP^{*}=AC\\cdot AC^{*}=AD\\cdot AD^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nTogether with (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP^{*}=AC^{*}=AD^{*}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(A\\) is the midpoint of the segments \\(P^{*}C^{*}\\) and \\(P^{*}D^{*}\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nP^{*}C^{*}\\perp AC^{*},\\qquad P^{*}D^{*}\\perp AD^{*}. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(C^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) and \\(D^{*}\\in\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\), (8) tells us that \\(C^{*}\\) (resp. \\(D^{*}\\)) is the foot of the perpendicular from \\(P^{*}\\) to \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) (resp. \\(\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\)).\n\nThe second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) is \\(E\\).  After inversion the line \\(AP\\) is still the line \\(AP\\); its second intersection with the line \\(\\ell_{\\Omega}\\) is the reflection of \\(C^{*}\\) in the line \\(AP\\).  Denote this reflection by \\(E^{*}\\).  Similarly, the second intersection with \\(\\Gamma\\) is the reflection of \\(D^{*}\\) in \\(AP\\); denote it by \\(F^{*}\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nAE^{*}\\perp P^{*}C^{*},\\qquad AF^{*}\\perp P^{*}D^{*}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Image of the orthocentre \\(H\\)\n\nLet \\(X\\) be the foot of the altitude from \\(P\\) to the side \\(MN\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  By definition of orthocentre, \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\) (equivalently in \\(NX\\)).\n\nBecause the line \\(MN\\) does **not** pass through the centre \\(A\\), its image under \\(\\mathcal I\\) is a circle; denote it by  \n\n\\[\n\\omega=\\mathcal I(MN).\n\\]\n\nThe circle \\(\\omega\\) passes through the images of the three points \\(A,M,N\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\omega\\text{ is the circle through }A,\\;M^{*},\\;N^{*}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe point \\(X\\) lies on \\(MN\\); let \\(X^{*}\\) be its image.  From (1) we have  \n\n\\[\nAX\\cdot AX^{*}=k .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AX\\perp MN\\), the inversion sends the line \\(MN\\) to a circle and the perpendicular through \\(A\\) to a **diameter** of that circle.  Hence \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\); in particular \\(X^{*}\\) is the antipode of \\(A\\) on \\(\\omega\\) and  \n\n\\[\nAX^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the line \\(MX\\).  It does not pass through \\(A\\); therefore its image is a circle passing through \\(A,M^{*},X^{*}\\).  Because \\(AX^{*}\\) is a diameter of \\(\\omega\\), the circle \\((A M^{*} X^{*})\\) coincides with the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  Consequently the image of the line \\(MX\\) is precisely the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  The same reasoning shows that the image of the line \\(NX\\) is also the line \\(AX^{*}\\).\n\nSince \\(H\\) is the reflection of \\(P\\) in the line \\(MX\\), after inversion the point \\(H^{*}\\) is the reflection of \\(P^{*}\\) in the line \\(AX^{*}\\).  Using (11) we may replace \\(AX^{*}\\) by the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\nH^{*}= \\text{reflection of }P^{*}\\text{ in }M^{*}N^{*},\\qquad\nP^{*}H^{*}\\perp M^{*}N^{*}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Right angles at \\(H^{*}\\)\n\nFrom (3) the lines \\(\\ell_{\\Omega},\\ell_{\\Gamma}\\) and the line \\(M^{*}N^{*}\\) all pass through the same point \\(B^{*}\\); consequently they are **concurrent** and share the direction of the original line \\(AB\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{\\Omega}\\parallel AB,\\qquad \\ell_{\\Gamma}\\parallel AB,\\qquad M^{*}N^{*}\\parallel AB. \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nUsing (8), (9) and (13) we compute\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},AB)\n   &=\\angle(E^{*}H^{*},P^{*}C^{*})+\\angle(P^{*}C^{*},AB)\\\\\n   &=90^{\\circ}+90^{\\circ}=90^{\\circ},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nand similarly \\(\\angle(F^{*}H^{*},AB)=90^{\\circ}\\).  Thus the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AB\\) (which is the same direction as \\(AP\\)) is perpendicular to both \\(E^{*}H^{*}\\) and \\(F^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Cyclicity of \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\)\n\nThe two opposite angles \\(\\angle(E^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\\) and \\(\\angle(F^{*}H^{*}B^{*})\\) are right angles; therefore the quadrilateral \\(B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}H^{*}\\) is cyclic and its diameter is the segment \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Tangency in the inverted picture\n\nIn a circle the radius through a point of the circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.  Since \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) is a diameter, the line through \\(H^{*}\\) that is **parallel** to the radius \\(AB\\) coincides with the diameter \\(B^{*}H^{*}\\) and is therefore tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\nBecause the direction of \\(AB\\) is the same as that of \\(AP\\) (both pass through the centre of inversion \\(A\\)), the line through \\(H^{*}\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Returning to the original configuration\n\nInversion preserves angles and tangency, and it fixes every line that passes through its centre \\(A\\).  Hence the pre‑image of the tangent line found above is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  The image of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) is the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle B^{*}E^{*}F^{*}\\); therefore the original line is tangent to the original circumcircle.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
